#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125190}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125191}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125192}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125224}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125726 :tutorial/content "The **gradient** of a multivariable function is analogous to the derivative of a single-variable function.\n\nFor a function $f(x,y)$ whose partial derivatives exist at a point $(x,y),$ the **gradient vector** is denoted $\\nabla f(x,y)$ and can be found by computing the vector of partial derivatives:\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\nabla f (x,y) &=\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x} \\mathbf i + \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y} \\mathbf j\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "4b8c5c84-83d9-55aa-bb25-87cf1904ed2e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2399 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125727 :tutorial/content "For a function of three variables $f(x,y,z)$, the gradient of $f$ is given by the vector  \n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\nabla \\! f (x,y,z) &= f_x(x,y,z)\\ \\mathbf i + f_y (x,y,z)\\ \\mathbf j + f_z (x,y,z)\\ \\mathbf k.\n                             \n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFor a function $f(x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n)$ of $n$ variables, its gradient is usually given as a column vector:\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n) = \n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}{f}_{ {x}_{1}} \\\\ {f}_{ {x}_{2}} \\\\ \\vdots  \\\\ {f}_{ {x}_{n}}\\end{array} \\right]}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "be2e3466-803d-5c6c-aeb9-f6cc57b000f8" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2400 :tutorial/title "The Gradient of a Function of More Than Two Variables"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125728 :tutorial/content "Since the gradient of a function of several variables is made up of partial derivatives, we can expect it to have properties that are similar to partial derivatives.\n\nAssume that $f$ and $g$ are functions whose first-order partial derivatives exist and are continuous on some open domain, and that $a,b,$ and $c$ are real constants. Then, the gradient has the following properties:\n\n- Constant rule:  $\\nabla c = \\mathbf 0$\n\n- Linearity: $\\nabla (af + bg) = a \\nabla\\! f + b\\nabla\\! g$\n\n- Product Rule: $\\nabla(fg) = g \\nabla\\! f + f \\nabla\\! g$\n\n- Quotient Rule: $$\n\\displaystyle \\nabla \\left( \\dfrac{f}{g}\\right) = \\dfrac{ g \\nabla\\! f - f \\nabla\\! g}{g^2}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "52036b8f-4d6f-5609-bbcf-fc34a51d9b98" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2401 :tutorial/title "Properties of the Gradient"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125731 :tutorial/content "We can extend our knowledge of limits to multivariable functions. We'll first consider the two-variable case. \n\nWe denote $\\mathbf{x}=(x,y)$ and $\\mathbf{x}_0 = (a,b)$ as a particular, fixed vector. Let the function $f(x,y)$ be defined in some neighborhood of $\\mathbf x_0.$ \n\nThen, the notation\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{\\mathbf{x} \\to \\mathbf{x}_0} f (x,y) = L\n$$\n\nmeans that the value of $f$ can be made as close as we want to $L,$ provided that $\\mathbf x$ is sufficiently close to $\\mathbf{x}_0.$\n\nA few things to note:\n\n- This definition of a limit also extends to functions of more than two variables in the natural way.\n\n- All of the rules set out by the algebra of limits are valid for functions of several variables.\n\nWe say that $f(x,y)$ is **continuous** at $(a,b)$ if\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{\\mathbf{x} \\to (a,b)} f (x,y) = f(a,b).\n$$\n\nSo, if a function is continuous at $(a,b),$ then the limit can be evaluated by simply plugging $(x,y) = (a,b)$ into the function.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's calculate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (2,1)}} \\dfrac{xy}{x^2+y^2}\n$$\n\nThis function is continuous for all $(x,y)$ with the exception of $(0,0)$ where the denominator vanishes. Since the point $(2,1)$ does not cause the denominator to vanish, we can calculate the limit by substituting $(x,y) = (2,1)$ into the function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (2,1)}\\frac{xy}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{2\\cdot 1}{{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}}=\\frac{2}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "eccfdda1-288a-5169-adde-a00163fa348f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2480 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125732 :tutorial/content "Recall that for a function of a single variable $f(x),$ if the left and right limits both exist and are equal at some point $x=a$, then this is sufficient to conclude that the limit exists at $x=a.$ \n\nSo, if we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to a^-} f(x) = \\lim_{x\\to a^+} f(x) = L,\n$$\n\nthen we may conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to a} f(x) = L.\n$$\n\nFor a multivariable function, the situation is not so straightforward. For a function of two variables $f(x,y),$ we may only conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{\\mathbf{x} \\to \\mathbf{x}_0} f(x,y) = L,\n$$\n\nif we can show that the limit is the same *for all possible paths* on which $\\mathbf x$ can approach $\\mathbf{x}_0.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "b4b46a0a-5b1c-58eb-aa32-dd0ccd57f7a4" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2482 :tutorial/title "The Definition of a Limit: Single Variable Functions vs. Multivariable Functions"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125733 :tutorial/content "Suppose we want to find the equation of the **tangent line** to the ellipse \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^2}{12} + \\dfrac{y^2}{4} =1\n$$\n\n at the point $P(3,1).$\n\nTo solve this problem, we could find the slope of the line using techniques from single-variable calculus. However, we can also solve the problem by computing the gradient of a two-variable function.\n\nLet's start by defining \n\n$$\nf(x,y) = \\dfrac{x^2}{12} + \\dfrac {y^2}{4}.\n$$\n\nThen, the given ellipse is the level curve  $f(x,y)=1.$\n\nNow, we know that $\\nabla \\! f(3,1)$ is a vector that's normal to the level curve $f(x,y)= 1$ at $P(3,1).$ So, it's also normal to the tangent line to our ellipse at $P.$\n\n![](images/3d/3dc75e8fd4083af1bf90f46b2e875d5d552bffdc5c1cfaeabebef70f79f00828.png)\n\nSuppose that $(x,y)$ is any point on that tangent line. Then $\\mathbf v=\\langle x - 3, y-1 \\rangle$ is a vector that's parallel to the tangent line. So $\\mathbf v$ must be also orthogonal to $\\nabla f(3,1),$ and since the dot product of two orthogonal vectors is zero, we have that\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(3,1)\\,\\cdot \\,\\langle x - 3, y-1 \\rangle = 0.\n$$\n\nNow, computing $\\nabla\\! f$ and evaluating it at the point $P(3,1),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,f(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{x}{6},\\frac{y}{2}⟩ \\\\ ∇\\,f(3,1) & =⟨\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the equation of our tangent, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,f(3,1)\\cdot ⟨x-3,y-1⟩ & =0 \\\\ ⟨\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨x-3,y-1⟩ & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(x-3)+\\frac{1}{2}(y-1) & =0 \\\\ (x-3)+(y-1) & =0 \\\\ x+y-4 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "7a9dede8-0672-56eb-9084-cf581e3fb05f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2496 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125734 :tutorial/content "Given a level curve $f(x,y) = f(x_0,y_0),$ a **tangent vector** is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf t(x_0,y_0) = f_y(x_0,y_0) \\ \\mathbf i - f_x(x_0,y_0) \\ \\mathbf j.\n$$\n\n \n\nTo understand why the above vector is tangent to the level curve, first recall that the gradient vector $\\nabla f(x_0,y_0)$ is normal to the level curve. Now, by computing the dot product, we see that the vector $\\mathbf t$ is orthogonal to the gradient vector $\\nabla\\! f{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,f\\cdot t & =({f}_{x}\\text{ }i+{f}_{y}\\text{ }j)\\cdot ({f}_{y}\\text{ }i-{f}_{x}j) \\\\  & ={f}_{x}{f}_{y}-{f}_{y}{f}_{x} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $\\mathbf t$ is tangent to the level curve." :tutorial/id #uuid "6ef07808-783a-543e-9217-5d3d0a9ae13a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2497 :tutorial/title "The Tangent Vector to a Plane Curve"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125735 :tutorial/content "Given a function $z=f(x,y)$, if both partial derivatives $f_x,$ $f_y$ exist and are continuous in a neighborhood of a point $P(x_0,y_0)$ in the domain of the function, and if $\\nabla \\! f(x_0,y_0) \\neq \\mathbf 0,$ then we have the following result:\n\n*$\\nabla \\! f(x_0,y_0)$ is a normal vector to the level curve $C$ that passes through the point $P.$* \n\nThis property is one of the most important facts in multivariable calculus since it gives a fundamental  link between\ncalculus and geometry!\n\nLet's illustrate this property with a concrete example. Consider the paraboloid $z = f (x, y)=  x^2 + y^2$ and the level curve $\\color{red}C$ of $f(x,y)$ corresponding to the point $P(1,-1).$  It is easy to see that  $\\color{red}C$ is a circle of radius $\\sqrt{2}$ with equation $x^2+y^2 = 2.$ \n\n![](images/f2/f2461dbfcde2403e6ed7de9d6288d33a4deb52319d35b7036628c3fa8b3789dc.png)\n\nIn this particular instance, the property says that\n\n*$\\nabla \\! f(1,-1)$ is a normal vector to the level curve $\\color{red}C$ that passes through the point $P(1,-1).$* \n\n![](images/48/481b68af522b9144e232d9c775f734cd998b31a21571cbfd983c23d0f68c3688.png)\n\nLooking at the situation in the $xy$-plane, we see the following:\n\n![](images/ce/ceb353d5164d6aa089a37e77c9469d747ef4430e363e42f9d35ee6d769d9a4e4.png)\n\nLet's verify that $\\nabla f(1,-1)$ is a normal line to the level curve $C$ at the point $P(1,-1).$\n\n- First, computing the gradient, we find that $\\nabla f = \\langle 2x, 2y \\rangle.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\nabla f(1,-1) = \\langle 2, -2 \\rangle.\n$$\n\n- Then, using elementary calculus, we can show that the equation of the tangent to $C$ at $P$ is $y=x-2.$ Since this line has slope $m=1,$ it follows that the vector $\\langle 1,1 \\rangle$  is parallel to the level curve.\n\n- Finally, taking the dot product between the gradient and the tangent vector, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(1,-1)\\cdot  \\langle 1,1 \\rangle = \\langle 2, -2\\rangle \\cdot \\langle 1,1 \\rangle = 0.\n$$\n\nThis means that $\\nabla f(1,-1)$ is *perpendicular* to the tangent line to $C$ at $(1,-1).$" :tutorial/id #uuid "68a96106-e224-5989-af04-8d464b6e58d5" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2499 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}]}
